#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711} :activity/id #uuid "8313edbb-dabc-5be6-9269-6a1622b71d3d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121049 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that a plane $\\Pi$ passes through the point $P(1,1,2)$ and that the vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle$ is perpendicular to the plane. Let's write down an equation of this plane using the dot product.\n\n![](images/0f/0f8353480099369ad92701d1799f72d46eab4bbe5c17add8cb37a4f34074311b.png)\n\nWe start by considering an arbitrary point $R(x,y,z)$ that lies on the plane. The position vectors of the points $R$ and $P$ are $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle$ respectively. So, the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =r-⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis parallel to the plane. Therefore, $\\overrightarrow{PR} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28b6770bd681257b1030679a421b80e3f3d1e8fbb86d2060b64c7301d0878c5c.png)\n\nNow, recall that if two nonzero vectors are perpendicular, then their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PR}\\perp \\mathbf{n} \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\overrightarrow{PR}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is called the **vector equation of the plane** that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}.$ In our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\langle 1,1,2 \\rangle) \\cdot \\langle 0,0,3 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using the properties of the dot product, we can arrive at an alternative vector equation given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot n & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c = \\mathbf{p} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}$ is a (scalar) constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "77d83d72-9202-50d7-af28-72062f00bfa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3255 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cf165cc4-ae6c-564d-b8e6-051c7aba1faa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f5c9c30-1745-5986-b116-e9e70a7c6161"} :step/id #uuid "e689c57c-a11c-582d-bed2-1b5777ad003d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee"} :step/id #uuid "36217cb6-2731-5a89-84ce-0738c511b417" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b"} :step/id #uuid "1b13bc4d-57be-5d17-841a-4f2645bc3982"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane with the following normal vector: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -1,2,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nLet's use the scalar product to find a vector that is parallel to this plane.\n\nWe denote our parallel vector as $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle.$ Now, since $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find a nonzero solution to this equation. To do that, we solve the equation for *one* of the variables (it doesn't matter which one). For example, let's solve our equation for the variable $t_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the variable $t_1$ depends on $t_2$ and $t_3,$ which, in turn, can be *any* real numbers. The variables $t_2$ and $t_3$ are called **free variables**.\n\nHence, the general form of our vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 2t_2+t_3, \\, t_2, \\, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_2, t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTo find a particular vector parallel to the plane, we can substitute any values for $t_2$ and $t_3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- For example, setting $t_2=0$ and $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1= \\langle 1, 0, 1 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Or, we could set $t_2=1$ and $t_3=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\langle 2, 1, 0 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n**Note:** There are infinitely many vectors parallel to a given plane. In our example, we found only two particular vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "980ef6f4-542d-5915-84b8-e34f4480ae68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6411 :tutorial/title "A Vector Parallel To a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "69d22458-d2d5-5c3d-b6fb-4714f2ea1b49" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120713}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120016} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420157} :activity/id #uuid "77095dee-04b2-5198-bba9-43f589fc9964" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043 :question/id #uuid "ca2b2271-1ec0-5a3e-b5ac-687d91668797"} :step/id #uuid "6262b847-bfad-5541-b7d9-0c48d99eb024" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262846 :question/id #uuid "aa3bed00-1af4-5c46-8c12-264209634c51"} :step/id #uuid "6f1edd60-137c-5af0-bf08-f3f3e9df0344" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420153 :question/id #uuid "3fa29ff6-5ae4-5642-82fd-03b1645e810c"} :step/id #uuid "7c175861-cf16-5bce-9a3d-7dd7700bfeb3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120224 :question/id #uuid "ee828cf7-8b80-575c-a644-51d699464b23"} :step/id #uuid "8d1b2442-6684-5f23-a6d5-881beb105644" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921864201