=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260760} {:answer/id #uuid "a344d6d0-c843-57a0-87ed-6643487a6ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-2,2,1⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261298} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b752a4-52ea-54e2-96dc-81dc14098d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,1⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261313} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9815dd-2b59-5495-91a4-c387e6882786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,-2⟩=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261371} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f71bee-c234-59c3-9e78-d739bf030e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩=15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261577} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e498358-75ca-5ef1-ae02-06dee6634086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262265}] :question/id #uuid "7885abf5-baa7-59dd-be20-bec3451c5be4" :question/math-academy-id "q-127956" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+2t \\\\ y &=3-2t \\\\ z &=5+t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+2t \\\\ y &=3-2t \\\\ z &=5+t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166ef2d2-32f9-5566-82ce-7cdba329c024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305351} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1369ba-d6af-518c-9a18-2bcb2250fd78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305582} {:answer/id #uuid "60c376f1-f147-5aa7-8c7c-ae55262553b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=⟨1,-4,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306084} {:answer/id #uuid "872e10d1-c31a-5bdc-a18f-00aeaf508a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩=⟨-1,5,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306439} {:answer/id #uuid "d788f251-5e9e-56e8-8b45-aa84f1099d81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-4,2⟩=-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩=-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305582} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fc16c6a-1a25-557e-b7df-b3323cb169a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307974}] :question/id #uuid "4553242a-e32b-5422-9dd6-251f47f5a320" :question/math-academy-id "q-33317" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,-4,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,6,-4⟩-⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f42755-b7a1-5809-87fc-9e17362d71bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩=⟨4,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336923} {:answer/id #uuid "682d20d3-364d-50e6-9603-4089e146e80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336962} {:answer/id #uuid "79bd5faa-c481-5160-a5a8-d577ea7ae9c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337127} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c6d7e8-9c47-5c5e-ac67-b2edb2098ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,3⟩=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337667} {:answer/id #uuid "bad32934-1f86-5ba6-bb6a-9563a6f86fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨4,-2,3⟩=⟨2,0,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336962} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebd3bcf-abcc-5e66-8e50-5b3cd1690259" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338502}] :question/id #uuid "ac6804de-8b54-5910-a3f3-3c7b3ad0197c" :question/math-academy-id "q-70893" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(4,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-1,0,1)$ and $B(1,0,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-1,0,1)$ and $B(1,0,-1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨2,0,-2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04e51591-bf02-5474-aec8-30aa3e188bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336071} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd38a34-b3ab-59ce-b0e6-a6d3b8de493e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336292} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb00c21-c6bb-5559-9485-37476b9643af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336442} {:answer/id #uuid "70fbd409-4407-5d02-94e5-220dccec345b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨2,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337038} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1a3637-079a-538d-96f7-2cf741de3741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336292} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ec79e2-ec6f-57fd-ace8-21faef1197d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338281}] :question/id #uuid "d76bf424-d04c-5aa9-b260-23e0fb3325ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-70894" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,1,-3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=2-t \\\\ z &=3+2t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=2-t \\\\ z &=3+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨1,-1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =⟨2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901763ea-91a4-5ccc-9fd1-7a5abd771549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387854} {:answer/id #uuid "e471dd7f-cf5c-5449-bdd5-1669c5ee7ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,3,2⟩=14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387855} {:answer/id #uuid "be245f9d-2743-5776-a695-325f9e3f765e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387856} {:answer/id #uuid "e50391f5-f264-5d75-924b-8343f68bda37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,2,2⟩=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387857} {:answer/id #uuid "9c146846-53c1-5f64-8789-a2b060a469a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,6,2⟩=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387856} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a27afd-b25c-559e-94cd-93712349e320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387853}] :question/id #uuid "78e0dc1f-5e12-54f0-b00c-492a979933d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-127937" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f8e54c-0615-5591-b542-0b782cd89daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391182} {:answer/id #uuid "de1fd91f-8a5e-51f3-8dc2-a58537f9f1b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391183} {:answer/id #uuid "c540887b-e7fe-5d55-972c-cf0a8340ba96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,0,4⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391184} {:answer/id #uuid "796aa680-c468-5852-b522-12b6bd2cf262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=⟨-3,0,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391185} {:answer/id #uuid "eee5c6a8-ae14-524b-a64c-c15d96be1896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,0,4⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391182} :answer-field/id #uuid "54786791-8d86-5133-85dc-e9b4fbb89dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391181}] :question/id #uuid "aa8c997f-15c5-5b2a-b1e2-bd26aa6233d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-33324" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-t \\\\ y &=4+3t \\\\ z &=-6+5t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-t \\\\ y &=4+3t \\\\ z &=-6+5t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,4,-6⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-1,3,5⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-1,3,5⟩.$ So, a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-1,3,5⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5ad7cf-e4ff-58ff-83bd-f3f78d2bf29c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393038} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5c0679-e0fe-53ca-b25e-60be64c06d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,-1⟩=⟨1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393039} {:answer/id #uuid "178672b3-37b3-5655-842f-d4da500e1495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=⟨2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393040} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a20d75-694b-5bf5-8c25-578546bfef99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨2,1,-1⟩=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393041} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c1542c-9778-5777-a91d-fc1ba92f6b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393038} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb32e226-7bfb-5486-b6de-65722871d5f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393037}] :question/id #uuid "be607052-6286-5c45-a7b0-2a7d8e65a24b" :question/math-academy-id "q-70892" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,1,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,1,-1⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,2,1⟩-⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493da2a3-c2b1-5d90-946c-32226dc19e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=⟨-3,4,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393045} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7fa3af-d391-569e-bc17-106f3e9396bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=⟨3,-5,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393046} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe2b282-7f73-5949-a7f3-cc5ddf1e2530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393047} {:answer/id #uuid "949e581b-c362-5470-828e-687788c962cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,-2⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393048} {:answer/id #uuid "1521f0fb-a511-5bc0-893e-2c6a06cceff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩=-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393049} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ef96c8-9198-5e30-8832-aa55a71dc6d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393044}] :question/id #uuid "ca2b2271-1ec0-5a3e-b5ac-687d91668797" :question/math-academy-id "q-70895" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,-5,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-3t \\\\ y &=3+4t \\\\ z &=4-t.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-5,-2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-3t \\\\ y &=3+4t \\\\ z &=4-t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,3,4⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨3,-5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c482fb6-1221-56a1-9d24-627eff7b967c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413448} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbbe94c-7a42-57a2-8bc5-73a5b3aa4afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413449} {:answer/id #uuid "8be31b94-69c9-5829-891b-14995d0113a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,2,3⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413451} {:answer/id #uuid "b346d644-6f1f-5ec8-a95e-b0ef3e5e25bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨1,2,3⟩=⟨-1,2,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413454} {:answer/id #uuid "c846c53c-a7c1-5cb9-84b1-15cf24c5ceca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413457} :answer-field/id #uuid "09321377-3d24-5a5f-8608-652765793fba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413459}] :question/id #uuid "8bddb63c-4c54-5e88-a8e9-1298e5060eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70883" :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,0)$ and $B(1,3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,0)$ and $B(1,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,3,5⟩-⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-1,2,5⟩.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩ & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,5⟩ & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1805 :topic/title "The Vector Equation of a Plane"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162851} :activity/id #uuid "b8d01ac6-d0ed-56fb-a724-8bf89dbf7d77" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163226 :tutorial/content "Given a bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ such that \n\n$$\nB:\\Bbb R^n \\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R\n$$\n\nand the corresponding $n \\times n$ matrix is *symmetric*, we can define the so-called **quadratic form** by substituting $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the new expression depends only on $\\mathbf{x}.$ So, a quadratic form is a function \n\n$$\nQ:\\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R.\n$$\n\nIn matrix notation, a quadratic form  can be given as \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. It's called the **matrix of the quadratic form**. \n\n**Watch out!** By definition, the matrix of a quadratic form must be *symmetric*.\n\nFor example, given the bilinear form \n\n![](images/99/996c4aa136dd7cda56787a2edb0f9481ed3d9356248a27afcde050ca613b6b9c.svg) \n\nthe corresponding quadratic form is then\n$Q( \\mathbf{x})=B( \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{x}).$\nExplicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Note**: In a quadratic form, all terms must be of degree two (considered as polynomials in the variables $x_1, x_2, \\cdots, x_n$). That's the reason we call it *quadratic*." :tutorial/id #uuid "f35d3e6c-6386-5c92-91c9-05fe3d888a47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4884 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "79f6f6cd-00ca-545a-aec0-257cfc16bf2b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9"} :step/id #uuid "c50bbdb7-ec08-57b4-bc96-b8e668bc34db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78"} :step/id #uuid "193a5105-f949-5b8b-a134-e0f453f3f2c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161986 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae"} :step/id #uuid "62f6605e-8903-5f4e-b4de-3b766aac20b8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1"} :step/id #uuid "f1a187e7-1f2b-511d-b52d-f35fecd8236e"}] :activity/title "Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162219}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161776 :question/id #uuid "122fd33e-5ef4-5200-9c72-67f40f3b8ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8168" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](images/52/528416f6f9bd58e04afc6df73ddffbe1e8645f1e94fc7113d1458fb806f08713.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2.$  Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}.$\n\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564c14f7-a788-59ff-9211-a424c70f2633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460389} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4c4b88-52ad-5d71-ab4d-57469fe441f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460390} :answer-field/id #uuid "21499751-6b1d-529b-a072-faa9a000656a" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0874b577-9056-52d7-81c1-efa4fb6206ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460391} {:answer/id #uuid "aee9b08e-d5b6-5bb0-9f15-0cf824b4a1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460391} :answer-field/id #uuid "62d5de62-98b7-579b-b597-fd1220fce7e1" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ac52e75-c49c-5d8f-9eb1-480d6c27a0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460393} {:answer/id #uuid "9173fadc-2738-59cb-b76a-10869c9357c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460393} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd644e5-7809-502b-a12b-6a4995d388ad" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461063}] :question/id #uuid "8ec77bfc-fd0e-5f6f-9e51-81a56de42ba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93700" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $Q$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $R$ is not symmetric since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c683e8-d022-5dee-bad9-35e2cd1ccd89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160989} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a2f5e8-88f2-5ca7-829a-bde5ff808860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160990} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a99bd4-fb67-538c-9ece-a4608cb1871a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160991} {:answer/id #uuid "fca7d285-ba33-51b7-8ce8-27bc0b3b6983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160992} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4a9a25-8245-5a1f-bdb8-7661bfd56e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160993} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd5c6c9-c655-5a1d-9014-f450a69f1b54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160988}] :question/id #uuid "6fea8541-6770-5397-b251-59a7fad8f7ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-94065" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $H$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $J$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $K$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $K$ is not symmetric since ${k}_{23}\\ne {k}_{32}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$H$ and $J$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355ee90c-29a8-5f7d-9858-d5d774afc427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464144} {:answer/id #uuid "52c13329-ed29-5a6a-9c3a-980f9b642801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464144} :answer-field/id #uuid "57268d9e-f878-521e-88c1-e38ad05c4b25" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fca2a6a-5f0e-57c2-b88a-83793aa0ea03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464146} {:answer/id #uuid "d9140ca1-8724-5815-b59d-81a85f001cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464146} :answer-field/id #uuid "d672360c-79a2-53e5-89fd-18eb4ebb73b0" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "729a3d56-7c5c-50c8-b093-a5ba7c6c0128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464148} {:answer/id #uuid "a192252a-3f86-5dc5-852c-3bafb620daf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464149} :answer-field/id #uuid "045f8e77-8eca-5ea2-86b3-e877ff4aa8cc" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465008}] :question/id #uuid "d68a509c-d790-5483-ab3b-63aef29c3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140870" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65093f23-78c7-51de-a363-9dbf483a2119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469668} {:answer/id #uuid "e3df6bc6-63f0-58f1-b185-9f86ffe8f132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469669} :answer-field/id #uuid "975b133f-d74b-51d2-abee-2ea32191fb78" :answer-field/key "K" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64b2294d-cee4-57bb-b2b3-e9f02a2402ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469670} {:answer/id #uuid "20eae9d6-2c44-536a-8eb2-c357b015ca69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469671} :answer-field/id #uuid "754fc5e3-20bb-552f-9086-00972a64ad00" :answer-field/key "L" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470447} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69f33c3f-be58-5c23-81dd-d2484c6e27b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469672} {:answer/id #uuid "4adc5058-528d-5d28-acec-3db27a965ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469672} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9d8951-1ed6-52a8-9476-