:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470454}] :question/id #uuid "61630213-687e-509d-b369-4384ce08371c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94063" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,E=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,F=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ has an associated $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $D$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}.$ Although it's a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $D$ is not symmetric since ${d}_{24}\\ne {d}_{42}.$\n\n- $E$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $4\\times 4$).\n\n- $F$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it is a $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$F$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40bedae6-4059-50c6-8adc-c9c715240d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ and $G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490635} {:answer/id #uuid "64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490699} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf84d03-66d8-568a-9b14-8f2ef4741a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490723} {:answer/id #uuid "77244729-5340-5f5f-9d6e-19fd434da41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ and $H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490748} {:answer/id #uuid "b7899cf8-c91b-54f9-9319-7ddeff060c06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490699} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8bb339-5bb9-5280-8b25-5509bc02dbb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491078}] :question/id #uuid "09cd1679-7967-5d9b-a465-697df00c6bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94061" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,G=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,H=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $F$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $F$ is not symmetric since ${f}_{12}\\ne {f}_{21}.$\n\n- $G$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $H$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$G$ only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161777 :question/id #uuid "d782c826-1111-5c83-98b6-220856d74b41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8169" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f85f2ebf91733fa3b5f0b6080da27be0ee4333ee2b7b27ab3fac2dad1acb2d41.svg)\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when ![](images/81/815a776cae60244113513dc20183d05caeece6aaef807fd65e5c4ac819f2d4d3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Substituting ![](images/e8/e8a68c7428583d7df9f7fb18faf50f315192475e2f9bb12d051ccc1fda5638a3.svg) into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 23\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =86.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c0f7ee-8257-5978-9995-847557603cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354886} {:answer/id #uuid "5a89aa8b-ca65-548d-b0dc-b5fc70dec25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355462} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f078fe-97a2-5b49-bdaa-fc00186879c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356268} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6c5274-b98b-5721-8f00-b472547ecdc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356304} {:answer/id #uuid "d6156e17-1955-543e-9283-6a83712e250d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355462} :answer-field/id #uuid "c91456ff-b3ca-5e8e-b645-9121f606dced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357279}] :question/id #uuid "2b88a995-140a-54a3-a483-210a7791965f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93703" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (-4)+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+0+16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c3f1e4-516c-542c-a770-a99ac27af0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160857} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ac8e04-476b-5b81-8729-db659870cca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160858} {:answer/id #uuid "11c55a8a-92c9-5cd8-81e5-b9f5f051fffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160859} {:answer/id #uuid "1a31e8cd-e910-54bb-a445-912f27c5747b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160860} {:answer/id #uuid "72ac6cf7-8dd2-55fe-b905-d6fa3f03cad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160861}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7c3599ff-c818-5820-8089-be1ec735bd20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160856}] :question/id #uuid "efffb0a2-d7e8-5eb3-af1d-7f3142ab312f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93704" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x) = x^{T}Ax$ when $x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03315f4c-29f9-5335-bf32-1a4c56087564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269297} {:answer/id #uuid "7c208796-7c48-534f-b951-cdeb5025345e" :answer