d}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{13} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ae9be6-e3e6-59d4-b281-a63c94591f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385847} {:answer/id #uuid "40049e3f-bffa-5f0e-a9c8-2bba4686c07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385848} {:answer/id #uuid "085d1d56-b1dd-5cbd-a4fb-cac8147c91c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385849} {:answer/id #uuid "96138058-3bf5-5f68-b326-4f584f2e0377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385850} {:answer/id #uuid "7a00dcda-5aaf-5a42-9073-65e57806a12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385848} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd61e26a-f154-5f32-82ad-e5ead117f18e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385846}] :question/id #uuid "0ea7fe9d-28ed-5f54-bf4a-f9c31ca64b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95307" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\,0 & \\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "684d0d7c-6c21-5d44-b7c3-71eab50414a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404789} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb76c1a-945d-5de9-8a45-072766c1ef8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3},\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404790} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a6892e-2b71-58c4-8195-ae2ebdcda46b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404791} {:answer/id #uuid "caa0c8c7-3e7b-52f8-87b0-6d3a1f9c7cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404792} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d3a286-9f2e-5033-b1c0-5b409e80ea4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3},\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404790} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a494b6c-01e8-55d5-b122-0aa7d1180b90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404788}] :question/id #uuid "ae342c4d-42b7-5fdb-833c-f679170c12f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95354" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1-\\lambda  & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ -(1-\\lambda )+(1-\\lambda )[(2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-1)] & =0 \\\\ -(1-\\lambda )+(1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +1-1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7b9b80-3654-5da0-af41-f1dd281a1ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,3,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404796} {:answer/id #uuid "adfd1028-f71d-5fb8-bf65-f6a904a4cad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404797} {:answer/id #uuid "421f5096-6ffe-58b8-95fd-205273c0d9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404798} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e32e2-6234-54e2-a995-a9f7bc9f67fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404799} {:answer/id #uuid "b769f855-028a-56bf-bb0f-d1970e2ab6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404798} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e48eb31-ad56-59b2-a6ab-bf3fcb90c449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404795}] :question/id #uuid "de321704-9fad-5ba9-910c-dac492b8f0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95377" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )[(8-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4)]+2(-2)(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4)+(-4)(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f16480c-d86a-5db9-b802-4ced71c8377c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5},\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415549} {:answer/id #uuid "5febb191-7c5d-5f4e-82d6-efc23b4d4485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16,\\,9,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415551} {:answer/id #uuid "73c9cf54-1f9b-52f1-b8ed-dd9e193c12b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,4,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415555} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3fcfba-14a5-5b55-ba3e-4a632953d6dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415560} {:answer/id #uuid "be6dbb3a-d9f4-5182-87d2-f89ac41af4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,\\,3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415560} :answer-field/id #uuid "b838eb18-ab2d-5fe6-b6c3-92ea8e153a8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415573}] :question/id #uuid "9ba2e577-1f2d-586f-a5db-7d03c7c71c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-95375" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}4-\\lambda  & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )[(5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-4)]-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +16)-16(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a190c4a-9cc2-50ab-a107-16fc3f92fc4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3},\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415545} {:answer/id #uuid "1e594ce1-22ea-5fd6-afb9-6b06b4b9255c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415546} {:answer/id #uuid "70323d57-c32e-523a-902e-eb0de24ca0c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415554} {:answer/id #uuid "83f12e6b-62c0-5595-a194-e0065cfb501d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415557} {:answer/id #uuid "c6eb0c7d-ecb7-57ee-81fb-a558588e214e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2},\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2},\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415564} :answer-field/id #uuid "646b1b63-2cd4-5c4d-b0e4-160d844e1954" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415571}] :question/id #uuid "9d417d43-c45e-500b-a5bf-a441848f66ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-95378" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$  \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c2115b7-3deb-582b-a761-e79b45a72ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3,$ $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416443} {:answer/id #uuid "72a19815-f7b7-56e9-badf-ea7b9e73091b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6,$ $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416453} {:answer/id #uuid "872eaed5-b7dd-5088-8dd0-359491c4f657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3,$ $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416457} {:answer/id #uuid "93dcd708-1476-51b0-ac1f-9db7ca101165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9,$ $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416459} {:answer/id #uuid "949e815d-00ae-5171-94b0-1798b1a30df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9,$ $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3,$ $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416443} :answer-field/id #uuid "533bf985-e003-5103-b11c-9c7a7c723cb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416477}] :question/id #uuid "ab4ca253-4533-55ea-9cf5-518c8864d1f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139508" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4889279a-9659-5dae-8259-b1442e0b8893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416445} {:answer/id #uuid "a08ff49c-3e48-5969-9cc0-4603170a0646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416462} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b05a8d-1f8d-58ee-8ab2-e2cf7750d0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416464} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d2dbc4-67b2-5124-9e27-6ec2f6fd08b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416466} {:answer/id #uuid "e93e9e61-f7d3-5cb6-85a3-a9059ed558a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416445} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7054d7-ed2b-5613-b2fc-90c17c4791c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416478}] :question/id #uuid "c791e385-b793-500d-9a77-3e468ca25a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-95359" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\lambda +40-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3819 :topic/title "Computing the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903} :activity/id #uuid "70093123-9441-504b-9d19-1ff886bae1a9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107157 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5bf1db5d-9d49-578a-b4a3-de51006d5ffd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "da72d9c9-d225-5f92-acf1-85ac68f4a4ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09"} :step/id #uuid "cec448b9-8490-5eef-b44a-d69c60421dab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e"} :step/id #uuid "a9e901bc-5996-547e-9050-8a27e0a73211" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107158 :tutorial/content "There is a geometric intuition behind the formula $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/93/93b062a23c613184e7cdf725852401168b609a32056a5efcd24ceca2703d81d2.png)\n\nTo get to any point $A(x,y,z)$ on the desired line, starting from the origin $O,$ we can\n\n- first travel along the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ to get to the point $P$ on the line, and\n\n- then travel along the vector $t \\mathbf{v}$ to get to $A.$ (The scalar $t$ represents how far we travel forward or backward along the line in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$)" :tutorial/id #uuid "24f7081a-5ffa-545e-9268-8abff5267044" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7712 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1882d34e-68eb-5861-b83e-9a6271a116a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba"} :step/id #uuid "7ec783db-e49b-5e4a-a359-af68f2d97438" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f"} :step/id #uuid "32a3a6c0-a3b3-531d-881f-278b4030a495"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107159 :tutorial/content "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ec7269d-cea3-5a82-818b-cb048217f704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7713 :tutorial/title "The Vector Equation of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "50416523-59b6-572d-9328-3ca6a25a4c94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106003} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431711} :activity/id #uuid "183eba74-280d-596d-84f9-614c47172a41" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431707 :question/id #uuid "5536a355-6822-5a47-a0a6-be384dfb79e8"} :step/id #uuid "385f8efa-dead-57c8-bb0d-ca58862d7bb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431709 :question/id #uuid "cf9c9d04-5ab5-558b-bf38-eae2eb3b3fea"} :step/id #uuid "62732027-4291-5ae0-9766-6825a84c1896" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431708 :question/id #uuid "c91c661d-e879-5f90-a5fb-ceb93bf19954"} :step/id #uuid "6b10edff-9a52-5f78-b02b-67d728b26c18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431706 :question/id #uuid "194bde74-7427-5189-95d5-f403b6acd3c5"} :step/id #uuid "90b2f552-3c11-524e-897e-0564ec54b233" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431705 :question/id #uuid "09c49afc-6085-50e6-863a-8dee0d62b478"} :step/id #uuid "983d1bf0-8aa7-5089-8bb5-ce39a8d426ba"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431674} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492105} :activity/id #uuid "cdd38161-b7a8-5f07-98fd-ab57d6d2c3c9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492050 :question/id #uuid "ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77"} :step/id #uuid "1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492072 :question/id #uuid "fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9"} :step/id #uuid "3b25e9df-26b3-594e-b7e5-ee4df36a2c1f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106066 :question/id #uuid "695c2a87-03bb-5036-ab74-93acc4cb34c8"} :step/id #uuid "4cb265da-0c2e-574b-9b97-7e10561660e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106283 :question/id #uuid "1704dfac-b6f1-5d12-b941-b68f37e7caca"} :step/id #uuid "bb116c13-04f6-5718-b5fc-2b6fa8103e9e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281392 :question/id #uuid "3bcbe167-633d-5f59-83f8-d10ba4510f50"} :step/id #uuid "fa1f6cbe-7cdd-5cc9-a956-7908cb978446"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491447}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105463 :question/id #uuid "9fa21896-8127-5ec5-aff0-7f811403db8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12442" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to ![](images/48/48f9afd751f6424fe465e494fd254593de065563231a1f5df07741789969c1af.png)" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is ![](images/92/9264ecd5e62e51eec08ae686461f8b3d99aadbddd2363b5c700c72839d59524f.png)\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc93209-4597-55c8-a0c1-3f5956e350af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321386} {:answer/id #uuid "38dbe2a1-ddd7-538f-a243-0cd0646fe9b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+t⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321580} {:answer/id #uuid "46b9726d-f469-55b5-be06-b88d42e22f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,-7⟩+t⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321704} {:answer/id #uuid "67932fff-19db-5512-8fd2-7596269e3abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+t⟨-7,\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321954} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eda061-d8bb-5a28-96db-2878fa4eeaf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+⟨-7,\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321386} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dba9b8c-60f6-5830-b5f6-c2a671a99f11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323266}] :question/id #uuid "554a7e69-f1e8-5d23-a1e8-d7437b02db1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35960" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7)$ and parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$ \n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce35b70-86c1-5895-b261-866548db5f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321366} {:answer/id #uuid "58d7a15b-e67b-506b-82f2-aadafb1986be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321846} {:answer/id #uuid "673b90db-44b0-5259-add3-cea949daf5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321952} {:answer/id #uuid "932b2654-f8cd-5471-99e1-887e6c735366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322312} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b4e111-f8da-554b-b32f-fb37ad290b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321952} :answer-field/id #uuid "060b61b0-1a43-5282-af35-58018ef0f881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323177}] :question/id #uuid "4388ff03-db93-521f-b780-3336d7d411a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-35966" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(-1,-4,5)$ and parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc1d975-18a9-5c3f-8a3a-614f1469da7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2⟩+t⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260287} {:answer/id #uuid "6177884e-229d-5b14-9c0c-f6ca746e7193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩+⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260712} {:answer/id #uuid "88b723d1-fa26-52f3-92dc-953c1ab0f710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩+t⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261065} {:answer/id #uuid "8be1b178-e492-56f9-bf2b-c93ecbc179d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩+t⟨-1,\\,2,\\,3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261097} {:answer/id #uuid "de9ee049-e8d6-5ba5-87dc-e1aa49071b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨2,\\,-1,\\,3⟩+t⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,-2,\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2⟩+t⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260287} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c77dd49-9011-5345-85fe-75e68e53b6f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262259}] :question/id #uuid "bb362192-da26-5954-90b7-9592c242b9f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126431" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2)$ and parallel to $v=⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$ \n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2⟩.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{5},\\,\\frac{3}{5},\\,-2⟩+t⟨2,\\,3,\\,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "648ac345-3bc2-5415-9944-ac5368f0f798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300471} {:answer/id #uuid "831c986d-17b3-5f3a-8555-20c3d415ccfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300613} {:answer/id #uuid "8f919c7f-5ffd-54fb-b8ff-262114724692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300688} {:answer/id #uuid "d6939b5b-2b27-57cf-8d32-61ef185ef7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301027} {:answer/id #uuid "f08cf76f-9833-50ed-a36d-a76c379221f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301027} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf6c7ed-5687-50bb-a895-c8361