gin{aligned}r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105464 :question/id #uuid "d1adb6fb-cd44-5a12-b892-6a6d2c99929d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12443" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,7⟩-⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab94b2c-a461-5b3a-b162-807789c39387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322488} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbc78b9-fb3c-5729-82f2-4229bf1502ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322738} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c7a26f-2ce7-5907-