asses through the points $A(2,-3,5)$ and $B(3,1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(2,-3,5)$ and $B(3,1,-2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-3,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,4,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a5c39e-5d72-5455-aec8-eb86a65caf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431681} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d5149a-691b-58ab-8849-b5bdde35f7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431688} {:answer/id #uuid "b4067977-bbce-543b-8574-d1b46a630254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431689} {:answer/id #uuid "dd35f8ed-d92f-5862-8188-f38211a0649f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431695} {:answer/id #uuid "ebce683f-51aa-5b02-8297-81246cd6de01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431689} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb53027-c7e0-55e6-85b6-4d8da4b5b3e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431704}] :question/id #uuid "cf9c9d04-5ab5-558b-bf38-eae2eb3b3fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-35972" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $M(-1,-2,-3)$ and $N(2,3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $M$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=m+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $m$ is the position vector of $M.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $M(-1,-2,-3)$ and $N(2,3,4)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{MN} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,4⟩-⟨-1,-2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076aefd7-f3ef-5e65-9a6e-fcef6787013e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491468} {:answer/id #uuid "31f26793-455b-5519-8bca-61ab48aea421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491554} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3dc15f-f6a0-5469-bd09-5e5682833602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491577} {:answer/id #uuid "45c99e89-541a-51a5-acd4-9fe79f6c950f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491586} {:answer/id #uuid "bafb0fb7-23f1-5dd9-8af3-0d84685adcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491776} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d4e9ea-3206-52e6-90bd-f7265f05e6b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491942}] :question/id #uuid "ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-126964" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $H$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=h+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $h$ is the position vector of $H.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{HS} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105465 :question/id #uuid "55ee3b10-087f-521e-ba37-12541e199408" :question/math-academy-id "e-12444" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1642ff3e-5a19-51f2-a5f5-ff014405f444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(2,5,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(0,1,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288181} {:answer/id #uuid "362be234-f6c1-5b80-865b-04fc025798c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288394} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3382c5-a020-58d4-9c16-5da3c394c6da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(4,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288872} {:answer/id #uuid "9c088178-0e50-57c3-baf6-66600abf039a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(5,3,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289082} {:answer/id #uuid "bf378d66-9502-5095-9b85-6dc355056369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(5,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,-1,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288394} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac287c57-fbbd-5635-8dea-451c8c888fad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290110}] :question/id #uuid "f95ebb39-0273-5ef9-b622-24560965b7f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-22291" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩)-⟨3,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩+1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨5,2,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩-1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca1ce6f-1cfa-5862-abce-de665c4b16ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-3,9,-4)$ and ${A}_{2}(-5,11,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491545} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8daf29-4004-5012-a711-688c5f2c743f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491695} {:answer/id #uuid "9028b822-283b-56d0-899a-4c8406bf3508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-3,9,-4)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-11,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491699} {:answer/id #uuid "92e3c2fb-3736-54ce-91a9-4ddf0df4c5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(4,-4,8)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,4,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491704} {:answer/id #uuid "aeea0b74-9973-5027-885f-71438bcfb05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,-7,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491695} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e72671-61e7-583b-875f-5e5b03441ae1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103846}] :question/id #uuid "1704dfac-b6f1-5d12-b941-b68f37e7caca" :question/math-academy-id "q-32819" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-2,5,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{24}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩)-⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{24},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{6{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{6} & =2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩+2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨0,3,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩-2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨-4,7,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c2c1ca-5234-51c3-8d36-535047693e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260356} {:answer/id #uuid "be7332cc-88ac-5e39-bef3-b311a756432b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261555} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb756d4-a702-5343-8aa3-33df1f54530d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261689} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c57590-f00f-5612-84a2-093278806a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(2,-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261740} {:answer/id #uuid "d800005a-fd5d-53a4-80c0-d5cd0fcbe588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(2,1,-2)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261555} :answer-field/id #uuid "833a2765-b262-5ff4-9cd5-1e1fd0778abf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262343}] :question/id #uuid "93d8d751-1660-5c0d-a647-5382ac8f5fb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-126991" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-1,0,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,1⟩+t⟨-1,0,2⟩)-⟨3,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{5},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ |⟨-t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-t)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+0+4{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,1⟩+⟨-1,0,2⟩=⟨2,1,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,1⟩-⟨-1,0,2⟩=⟨4,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(2,1,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,1,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb0aa2d-8a90-5360-8230-e32b30547013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259984} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0912e3-7ddd-54fa-b90d-25ccc6642f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260420} {:answer/id #uuid "524783d8-0286-5105-b49a-d96c826c4f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(-5,3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260570} {:answer/id #uuid "ec495249-b99d-58d5-8d05-a8f15f13cf5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,-5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261948} {:answer/id #uuid "f32f71e0-a972-57b4-b2bb-ee9147e660e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262000} :answer-field/id #uuid "aac9c7f3-ee54-59ef-bcec-0b316e5a9647" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262429}] :question/id #uuid "01976ddb-a3c2-5d6a-8e10-0f4fa56cdf01" :question/math-academy-id "q-126992" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,-2,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-2,1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩)-⟨3,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩+⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨1,-1,5⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩-⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨5,-3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d95f4fb-4412-5bf5-9464-291c5abc6946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}(4,2,2)$ and ${R}_{2}(-3,-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333417} {:answer/id #uuid "58e95567-acf3-5b36-9352-d8ebac44038c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}(3,4,4)$ and ${R}_{2}(3,-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333802} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6cc3ec-9172-562a-8354-2eb20f2842ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}(4,3,3)$ and ${R}_{2}(2,3,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334225} {:answer/id #uuid "d08d6d20-fa42-5eb5-9a24-a87effb51f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}(4,-3,-3)$ and ${R}_{2}(2,3,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334813} {:answer/id #uuid "e2df4a11-9581-5535-880c-dabf98f19c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}(4,3,3)$ and ${R}_{2}(-2,-3,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{1}(4,3,3)$ and ${R}_{2}(-2,-3,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334964} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8778f7-427d-5068-9dde-712bbd2bcf14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335328}] :question/id #uuid "fbd6fb67-c5d3-5178-a4fb-68767de900d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-69783" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,0,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ Find all the possible points $R$ on that line such that $RP=3\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,0,0⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $R$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =⟨1,0,0⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,0,0⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩)-⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PR=3\\sqrt{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RP & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PR}| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3{t}^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 3$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{R}_{1}}=⟨1,0,0⟩+3⟨1,1,1⟩=⟨4,3,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{R}_{2}}=⟨1,0,0⟩-3⟨1,1,1⟩=⟨-2,-3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${R}_{1}(4,3,3)$ and ${R}_{2}(-2,-3,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345297 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a3d022-861f-53e7-a025-94113f95ddd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(10,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(2,6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343082} {:answer/id #uuid "6c61eeb1-ac06-543e-92c9-3bb080cd736b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(10,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(7,6,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343165} {:answer/id #uuid "b4571a9e-ee26-522a-bcd9-06d3f1d7727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(8,-12,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(7,6,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343820} {:answer/id #uuid "c69b84ed-b24f-5e12-8528-c392edf7e56a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(6,-6,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(2,6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343980} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3893ca-f82c-58f9-8709-7dbcfa0a1f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(6,-6,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(10,18,8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(6,-6,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(2,6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343980} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff5f0fb0-e680-51ca-bfe1-71b96b0112cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344942}] :question/id #uuid "51874db2-0a24-5260-b569-2d6f8cdd89f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-77442" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(4,0,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,-6,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨4,0,-1⟩+t⟨2,-6,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨4,0,-1⟩+t⟨2,-6,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨4,0,-1⟩+t⟨2,-6,3⟩)-⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨2,-6,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-6t,\\,3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=7,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =7 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =7 \\\\ |⟨2t,\\,-6t,\\,3t⟩| & =7 \\\\ \\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-6t)^{2}+(3t)^{2}} & =7 \\\\ \\sqrt{49{t}^{2}} & =7 \\\\ \\pm 7t & =7 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨4,0,-1⟩+⟨2,-6,3⟩=⟨6,-6,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨4,0,-1⟩-⟨2,-6,3⟩=⟨2,6,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(6,-6,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(2,6,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4dba27-04b2-5c7c-a0f4-65dfb83678d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400277} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fa9a5b-f01b-53ec-95e1-597f44339eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400278} {:answer/id #uuid "b58591df-e0d8-522a-a4de-ebba4b8f0e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400279} {:answer/id #uuid "358f67ac-2cdf-5196-b078-18b1f8cb7e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400280} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeeb864-562e-5444-a903-bc04d0d2fb6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400278} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8304411-0971-51d5-bb97-446c83b8db6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400276}] :question/id #uuid "59a52ddc-339d-53d5-a5c4-8b8787ceb738" :question/math-academy-id "q-126995" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(2,3,6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,-2,1⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩)-⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-3t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨2,3,6⟩+⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨-1,1,7⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨5,5,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2d05b78-acf4-5f5e-a4a5-a47b385be2af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(6,2,2)$ and ${Q}_{2}(1,0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403873} {:answer/id #uuid "e86a9368-3e01-579c-a45b-27cec0740826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(4,2,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(1,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403874} {:answer/id #uuid "243227bd-445a-5c74-9b23-fe624173fb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403875} {:answer/id #uuid "0312db3e-2587-592d-bac1-fd0950f055c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(-1,3,-9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403876} {:answer/id #uuid "d156c13a-c459-55bc-8fae-a2511dac06a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(3,-1,11)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403875} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e8713f5-ca93-55c6-99f3-5140f5065e48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403872}] :question/id #uuid "ca1265ac-23c6-5e5b-be9c-b56a47160343" :question/math-academy-id "q-77443" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,1,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,-1,5⟩.$ Find all the possible points $Q$ on that line such that $PQ=3\\sqrt{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Q$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩)-⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,5t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PQ=3\\sqrt{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,5t⟩| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(5t)^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{27{t}^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\pm 3\\sqrt{3}t & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Q}_{1}}=⟨1,1,1⟩+⟨1,-1,5⟩=⟨2,0,6⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Q}_{2}}=⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨1,-1,5⟩=⟨0,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1339a533-dfd9-5aa8-8328-6269486f9c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,3,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,4,-5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431675} {:answer/id #uuid "38ce3564-96d6-5aa9-8f31-aca072b73313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(3,2,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,5,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431676} {:answer/id #uuid "9124bc93-158c-5515-bd32-81970e107298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431684} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbd0720-72fa-5325-aa30-34ea538948f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431693} {:answer/id #uuid "e55a4e80-0a0f-5330-8c1a-123763b280cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431684} :answer-field/id #uuid "9028126a-fede-53d4-8337-146b30a620e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431702}] :question/id #uuid "194bde74-7427-5189-95d5-f403b6acd3c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126965" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,3,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-4,-2,4⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,3,1⟩+t⟨-4,-2,4⟩)-⟨3,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,-2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,-2t,\\,4t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,-2t,\\,4t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(4t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{36{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,3,1⟩+\\frac{1}{2}⟨-4,-2,4⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,3,1⟩-\\frac{1}{2}⟨-4,-2,4⟩=⟨5,4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,2,3)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,4,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416c72c0-8fb5-5d68-8b29-8af5bf68ead0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(14,7,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-5,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431677} {:answer/id #uuid "4af680d4-f96a-54d6-a42c-84ed0f82803b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,12,2)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-10,-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431679} {:answer/id #uuid "992d7033-f50d-5a79-9ab6-cbf67a0e1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431685} {:answer/id #uuid "c5acab05-2c88-5065-9ff0-9e18e32d1ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-5,-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431690} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c7a32e-0763-553a-ae8f-36a7709abe9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,12,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-5,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431685} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4fda4c-a5fe-531d-a4cc-08d2ea5d8a53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431703}] :question/id #uuid "c91c661d-e879-5f90-a5fb-ceb93bf19954" :question/math-academy-id "q-69807" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,2,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩.$ Find all the possible points $Y$ on that line such that $YP=\\sqrt{160}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Y$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OY}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩)-⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PY=\\sqrt{160},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YP & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PY}| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{10}t & =4\\sqrt{10} \\\\ t & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 4$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{1}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨7,14,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{2}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩-4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨-1,-10,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bce919e-a0d8-59f0-8778-d5b453d280a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(2,-2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-4,-2,-5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491510} {:answer/id #uuid "44125a3b-2563-5069-8bbc-ae69a62e4b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(1,2,-1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,3,-9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491584} {:answer/id #uuid "53ee61a5-d475-57b8-a0b9-a60723219d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491614} {:answer/id #uuid "bf682358-c577-5e53-95cb-2e1a7d3008bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,0,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(1,2,-8)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491783} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f87627-6a47-5a19-b342-cf1d6b482b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,4,-1)$ and ${X}_{2}(2,4,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491614} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db99a8f-cee5-52ce-ae30-3cf87758843e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491919}] :question/id #uuid "fae33bcb-2652-544e-838d-9fea7f4029a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-69799" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-4,2,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $X$ on that line such that $XP=\\sqrt{20}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $X$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩)-⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PX=\\sqrt{20},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XP & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PX}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{1}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-2,2,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{2}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩-2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105466 :question/id #uuid "69c3fc44-8b4d-521e-b207-0ea01d5921e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12445" :question/problem "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+5\\,j-k)-(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(4t+1)\\,j+(2-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "889ec9d2-69c7-5777-a4ab-83be88b6c805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102277} {:answer/id #uuid "a64fd557-d9a3-530f-b691-942e3528d54f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102278} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9ec853-8db4-5ead-9378-e1bdc7bd390f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102279} {:answer/id #uuid "1271a0be-466c-5ae6-8731-8a416fb037f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102280} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3a93bc-7771-523b-8bea-9fd3d62ac833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102281}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ef625fff-1c69-5d10-93fe-ed07c78cdd97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102276}] :question/id #uuid "0442931a-1239-5c77-9d14-4b1720805336" :question/math-academy-id "q-162143" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $PQ^(―)$, where $P(1, 4,-2)$ and $Q(-2, 3, 1)$.\n$r(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ [0, c]$\nWhat is the value of $a + b + c$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c0bd9a-8112-53b2-b018-8f7e3b0e9cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491492} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddce68d-485d-5026-a8d0-15c7aadcd2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491652} {:answer/id #uuid "88a898e9-97f9-5c0d-a2a9-e984a3785f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491686} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4c0811-7dac-5fed-9809-20000ee340e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491698} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7163c1-8542-5ca0-8417-6dfd8942bd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491686} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca2dac9f-c1e9-524d-9d24-eaf747039476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102282}] :question/id #uuid "695c2a87-03bb-5036-ab74-93acc4cb34c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162144" :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,0,3)$ and $Q(1,2,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)-(i+3\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+(3-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2t+(3-3t)+0=3-t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34dc3d85-bf7d-5a32-bc17-8ac750c355ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279049} {:answer/id #uuid "642b22ea-1596-5521-b3ff-b9cef628875f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279502} {:answer/id #uuid "846472d7-37b6-51a7-af37-dc5316fa1feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279779} {:answer/id #uuid "9500f03a-d333-5927-bed1-97f253541b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279916} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb56e0e-2818-55a9-ad7f-03187608868a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280532} :answer-field/id #uuid "678b25a4-44dd-597e-88cf-490c50666c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281013}] :question/id #uuid "d31dc5a7-37f0-52db-ac08-e744b2aa0398" :question/math-academy-id "q-162161" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bma